<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Lima e Silva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1785">1785</a>–<a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> e Silva was a Brazilian general and politician. In 1824 he commanded the imperial forces that suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a> in Pernambuco. After the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> in 1831 he served on the regency that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> during the minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>. His son became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-alves-de-lima-e-silva-duke-of-caxias">Duke of Caxias</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-lima-e-silva.jpg" alt="Francisco de Lima e Silva" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sébastien Auguste Sisson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_de_Lima_e_Silva_-_Litografia_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
